Geographic Tongue (Benign Migratory Glossitis)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Migratory smooth red tongue patches, Raised serpiginous white borders, Loss of filiform papillae, Sensitivity to spicy foods

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-020
Reason for Visit: Acute panic and severe distress regarding a rapidly changing, sensitive rash on the tongue, feared to be a malignant lesion or severe infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a highly anxious patient reporting rapidly evolving, painful red lesions on the tongue with white borders. The patient was acutely distressed, unable to eat due to pain, and fearful of oral cancer or acute aggressive infection. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively visually inspect the oral cavity, exclude acute mucosal ulceration or deep infection, and provide immediate reassurance to prevent an inappropriate presentation to the emergency department prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My tongue is covered in these burning red bald patches with white outlines that are moving every day, I'm terrified it's mouth cancer."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 1-week history of irregular, well-demarcated smooth red patches on the dorsal tongue. The patient is highly distressed because the lesions are migratory, changing pattern and location over a period of days. The patient reports a distinct sensitivity and mild burning sensation strictly localized to the patches, entirely exacerbated by eating a spicy curry tonight. The patient has been checking their tongue hourly. There is a complete absence of deep ulceration, frank bleeding, or difficulty swallowing.
Functional Status: Independent, but completely avoiding oral intake tonight due to intense fear and hypersensitivity.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound acute health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, anxious, frequently protruding tongue in front of the mirror.
Physical Exam: Oral examination reveals multiple, irregular, well-demarcated smooth red patches on the dorsal and lateral aspects of the tongue. There is a distinct loss of filiform papillae strictly within these erythematous zones. The patches are surrounded by raised, distinct, serpiginous white and yellowish borders. Complete absence of induration, deep ulceration, frank bleeding, or purulent exudate. No cervical lymphadenopathy. Tonsils and posterior pharynx are entirely clear. Co-occurrence of mild fissuring on the central tongue.
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten spicy takeaway meal on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Geographic Tongue (Benign Migratory Glossitis) - Status: Acute symptomatic flare
Differentials:

Oral Candidiasis - Ruled out as the white borders are not removable pseudomembranous plaques, and the red areas are distinct areas of depapillation, not diffuse erythema.

Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out clinically by the lack of induration, absence of deep non-healing ulceration, and the rapid migratory nature of the lesions (cancers do not migrate daily).

Oral Lichen Planus - Unlikely due to the lack of classic bilateral lacy white striae (Wickham striae) on the buccal mucosa.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to acute, severe health anxiety and a refusal to maintain oral intake secondary to painful, rapidly evolving oral lesions. The visual appearance and rapid progression of the lesions triggered an intense fear of malignancy. An immediate physical examination of the oral cavity was necessary to definitively clinically exclude an acute infectious process, deep ulceration, or indurated mass. By visually confirming the classic, completely benign morphology of Geographic Tongue, the physician immediately resolved the acute psychological crisis and prevented an imminent, highly inappropriate emergency department presentation, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided direct, firm clinical reassurance regarding the completely benign and non-malignant nature of the condition. Advised strictly avoiding spicy, highly acidic, or exceptionally salty foods during active flares.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink a glass of cold water or chew plain gum immediately after eating. The rapid clearance of food debris and mechanical soothing will instantly reduce the sensory burning in the depapillated areas, reinforcing the importance of oral hygiene over catastrophizing about the lesions.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks for baseline nutritional blood panels (B12, Iron, Folate) as a standard precaution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing rapidly progressive swelling of the tongue or lips, any difficulty breathing, or inability to swallow saliva.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; if lesions become deeply ulcerated, bleed continuously, or develop a hard lump, prompt dental or medical review is mandatory.
Clinical Course: Geographic tongue is a completely harmless, inflammatory condition of the tongue's surface. It does not cause cancer. The patches will migrate and eventually disappear, though they frequently recur.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your tongue or lips swell up quickly, if you have any trouble breathing, or if a hard, bleeding sore develops that does not heal.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The top layer of your tongue is temporarily losing its tiny bumps (papillae) in patches, exposing the sensitive tissue underneath. This makes it acutely sensitive to acid and spice, but it is not an infection or a tumor.
Actionable Steps: Completely avoid tomatoes, citrus, and spicy foods until the red patches fill back in. Eating plain, non-irritating foods will allow the mucosal surface to heal faster, directly demonstrating that you have control over the sensitivity.

